{:edb/db-before-t 1626 :edb/db-after-t 1627 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534939 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:34:23.286Z" 13194139534939 true] [17592186124288 1145 "The rate at which the fish population $P(t)$ of the lake changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum fish population the lake can support and the current fish population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(a - P)\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum fish population the lake can support, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\na = 200\\,\\textrm{fish}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(200 - P)\n$$" 13194139534939 false] [17592186124288 1145 "The rate at which the fish population $P(t)$ of the lake changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum fish population the lake can support and the current fish population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(a - P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum fish population the lake can support, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 200 \\textrm{ fish},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d P}{\\textrm d t} = k(200 - P).\n$$" 13194139534939 true] [17592186124289 1100 "In an aquarium, there are initially $20$ fish. After $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50$. Assuming inhibited growth, calculate the number of fish after $100$ weeks if the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $90$ fish." 13194139534939 false] [17592186124289 1100 "In an aquarium, there are initially $20$ fish. After $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ Assuming inhibited growth, calculate the number of fish after $100$ weeks if the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $90$ fish." 13194139534939 true] [17592186124289 1145 "The rate at which the number of fish $N(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the aquarium's maximum capacity and the current number of fish. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k\\left(a-N\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 90$ fish, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(90-N)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dN}{90 - N} &= kdt \\\\\n\\int \\frac{dN}{90 - N} &= \\int kdt \\\\\n- \\ln \\mid 90 - N \\mid &= kt + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $90 - N > 0$ for all\n\n$$\nt \\ge 0\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $N(t)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n90 - N &= e^{-kt - C} \\\\\n90 - N &= e^{-C}e^{-kt} \\\\\n90 - N &= Ke^{-kt} \\\\\nN &= 90 - Ke^{-kt}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial number of fish was $20$. So, we apply the initial condition $N(0)=20$, and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(0) &= 20 \\\\\n90 - Ke^{0} &= 20 \\\\\n90 - K &= 20 \\\\\nK &= 70\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $N(t)$:\n\n$$\nN(t)=90 - 70 e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50$. So, applying the condition $N(30)=50$, we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(30) &= 50 \\\\\n90 - 70e^{-30k} &= 50 \\\\\n40 &= 70e^{-30k} \\\\\ne^{-30k} &= \\frac{4}{7} \\\\\n- 30k &= \\ln (\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\\nk &= -\\frac{1}{30}\\ln (\\frac{4}{7})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nN(t)=90-70e^{(t/30)\\,\\ln\\left(4/7\\right)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of fish after $t=100$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nN(100) &= 90 - 70e^{(100/30)\\ln (4/7)} \\\\\n&= 79.161\\ldots  \\\\\n&\\approx 79\\,\\mathrm{fish}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139534939 false] [17592186124289 1145 "The rate at which the number of fish $N(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total number of fish and the current number of fish. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}N}{\\mathrm{d}t}=k\\left(a-N\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a = 90$ fish, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d} N}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(90-N).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dN}{90-N} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dN}{90-N} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|90-N| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $90 - N > 0$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $N(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90-N & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 90-N & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 90-N & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ N & =90-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial number of fish was $20.$ So, we apply the initial condition $N(0)=20,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(0) & =20 \\\\ 90-K{e}^{0} & =20 \\\\ 90-K & =20 \\\\ K & =70\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $N(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nN(t)=90 - 70 e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $30$ weeks, the number of fish is $50.$ So, applying the condition $N(30)=50,$ we can solve for $k{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(30) & =50 \\\\ 90-70{e}^{-30k} & =50 \\\\ 40 & =70{e}^{-30k} \\\\ {e}^{-30k} & =\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ -30k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{7}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{30}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nN(t)=90-70e^{(t/30)\\,\\ln\\left(4/7\\right)}.\n$$\n\nFinally,  the number of fish after $t=100$ weeks is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(100) & =90-70{e}^{(100/30)\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4/7)} \\\\  & =79.161… \\\\  & ≈79\\,\\text{fish},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139534939 true] [17592186124291 1100 "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0$, which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n4. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" 13194139534939 false] [17592186124291 1100 "Given that $y \\lt 60$ and $t \\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4y^2 - 240$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4 - 240y$" 13194139534939 true] [17592186124291 1145 "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ for all\n\n$$\nt \\ge 0\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4(60-y)\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4$, and $a=60$. Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y \\lt 60$ for all\n\n$$\nt \\ge 0\n$$" 13194139534939 false] [17592186124291 1145 "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ for all $t \\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 240 - 4y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 4(60-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=4,$ and $a=60.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y \\lt 60$ for all $t \\ge 0.$" 13194139534939 true] [17592186124594 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321232 13194139534939 true] [17592186124594 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321239 13194139534939 true] [17592186124594 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321246 13194139534939 true] [17592186124595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321176 13194139534939 true] [17592186124595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321183 13194139534939 true] [17592186124595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321211 13194139534939 true] [17592186124595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321218 13194139534939 true] [17592186124595 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321225 13194139534939 true] [17592186124596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321190 13194139534939 true] [17592186124596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321197 13194139534939 true] [17592186124596 1020 #edb/ref 17592186321204 13194139534939 true] [17592186124681 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534939 false] [17592186124681 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534939 true] [17592186124681 1100 "Given that $y < 80$ and $t \\ge 0$, which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" 13194139534939 false] [17592186124681 1100 "Given that $y<80$ and $t\\ge 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited growth equation?" 13194139534939 true] [17592186124681 1145 "An inhibited growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=400-5y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(80-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and $a=80.$ Note that for inhibited growth, we must have $y<80$ for all $t\\ge 0.$" 13194139534939 true] [17592186124681 1145 "Factor $400-5y=5(80-y)$. Thus $y^{\\prime}=400-5y$ is inhibited growth with positive constant $k=5$ and equilibrium $80$. Since $y<80$, the solution increases toward $80$." 13194139534939 false] [17592186124682 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534939 false] [17592186124682 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534939 true] [17592186124682 1145 "An inhibited growth initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=k(a-y),\\,y(0)={y}_{0}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, the only inhibited growth problem is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=150.\n$$\n\nIn this case, $k=3,a=250,$ and ${y}_{0}=150.$ Notice that $a>{y}_{0},$ which is required for inhibited growth. \n\nThe other options do not represent inhibited growth. In particular, the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=3(250-y),\\,y(0)=400,\n$$\n\ndoes *not* represent inhibited growth model because ${y}_{0}=400>a=250.$" 13194139534939 true] [17592186124682 1145 "The required form is $y^{\\prime}=k(a-y)$ with $k>0$ and an initial value below $a$. The equation $y^{\\prime}=3(250-y)$ has equilibrium $250$, and $y(0)=150<250$. Its solution is $y(t)=250-100e^{-3t}$, which increases toward $250$." 13194139534939 false] [17592186124712 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534939 false] [17592186124712 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534939 true] [17592186124712 1100 "A University Campus has a total of $1500$ students.\nAt the beginning of the new semester at the University, flu affected $300$ students. After $10$ days, the number of students affected by the flu increases to $500$. To the nearest day, how long does it take for the flu-stricken campus population to reach $800$ students?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of students affected by the flu, $P(t)$, increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t \\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139534939 false] [17592186124712 1100 "A University Campus has a total of $1\\,500$ students.\nAt the beginning of the new semester at the University, flu affected $300$ students. After $10$ days, the number of students affected by the flu increases to $500.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the flu-stricken campus population to reach $800$ students?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of students affected by the flu, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139534939 true] [17592186124712 1145 "The model is $P^{\\prime}=k(1500-P)$, so $P(t)=1500-Ce^{-kt}$. From $P(0)=300$, $C=1200$. Using $P(10)=500$ gives $e^{-10k}=1000/1200=5/6$, hence $k=\\ln(6/5)/10$. To reach $800$ students, $e^{-kt}=(1500-800)/1200=7/12$. Therefore $t=\\frac{10\\ln(12/7)}{\\ln(6/5)}\\approx29.563$ days, or $30$ days after the start." 13194139534939 false] [17592186124712 1145 "The rate at which the campus population affected by the flu, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total campus population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total population of the campus, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=1\\,500$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(1\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{1\\,500-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{1\\,500-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1\\,500-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $1\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,500-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 1\\,500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 1\\,500-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =1\\,500-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial campus population affected by the flu was $300$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=300,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =300 \\\\ 1\\,500-K{e}^{0} & =300 \\\\ 1\\,500-K & =300 \\\\ K & =1\\,200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the campus population affected by flu was $500$ students. So, applying the condition $P(10)=500,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =500 \\\\ 1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-10k} & =500 \\\\ 1\\,000 & =1\\,200{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $800$ students are affected by the flu. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =800 \\\\ 1\\,500-1\\,200{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =800 \\\\ 700 & =1\\,200{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{7}{12} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $800$ students will be affected by the flu after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{12})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{6})}=29.562…≈30\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." 13194139534939 true] [17592186124985 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534939 true] [17592186124985 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534939 false] [17592186124985 1100 "The population of students $P(t)$ in a school can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in years. If the initial population of the school is $800$ students, calculate the population after $4$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139534939 true] [17592186124985 1100 "The population of students $P(t)$ in a school can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dP}{dt} = 0.016(2500 - P)$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in years. If the initial population of the school is $800$ students, calculate the population after $4$ years. Round your answer to the nearest integer." 13194139534939 false] [17592186124985 1145 "Separating the equation gives $P(t)=2500-Ce^{-0.016t}$. Since $P(0)=800$, $C=1700$. Therefore $P(4)=2500-1700e^{-0.064}\\approx905.392$, so the population rounds to $905$ students." 13194139534939 false] [17592186124985 1145 "We're told that the population in the school is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.016(2\\,500-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =0.016\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{2\\,500-P} & =\\int 0.016\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2\\,500-P| & =0.016t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $2\\,500-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2500-P & ={e}^{-0.016t-C} \\\\ 2500-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ 2,500-P & =K{e}^{-0.016t} \\\\ P & =2\\,500-K{e}^{-0.016t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial population of the school was $800$ students. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=800,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800 & =2\\,500-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 800 & =2\\,500-K \\\\ K & =1\\,700\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nP(t)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the population in the school after $t=4$ years is\n\n$$\nP(4)=2\\,500-1\\,700{e}^{-0.016\\cdot 4}≈905\\,\\text{students},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139534939 true] [17592186321176 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321177 13194139534939 true] [17592186321176 1089 "q-335135" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321176 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534939 true] [17592186321176 1100 "A wildlife manager noted that the growth of the number of deer in a protected forest is directly proportional to the difference between the forest’s maximum deer population and the current population. Let $N(t)$ be the deer population, measured in number of deer, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum deer population that the forest can support is $150$ deer, which differential equation could be used to model the forest’s deer population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534939 true] [17592186321176 1113 #uuid "100642f2-5b37-581c-b6bb-cef2fcf7d983" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321176 1145 "The rate at which the deer population $N(t)$ of the forest changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum deer population the forest can support and the current deer population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-N),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum deer population the forest can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=150\\text{ deer},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N).\n$$" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321178 13194139534939 true] [17592186321177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321179 13194139534939 true] [17592186321177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321180 13194139534939 true] [17592186321177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321181 13194139534939 true] [17592186321177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321182 13194139534939 true] [17592186321177 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534939 true] [17592186321177 1041 "selection" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321177 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321182 13194139534939 true] [17592186321177 1110 #uuid "db12b224-d872-5232-af52-551132bbf28b" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321178 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-k(150+N)" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534939 true] [17592186321178 1111 #uuid "5c487e02-f26a-56aa-b7c5-bc5d1ca412e9" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321179 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=kN(150-N)" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321179 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534939 true] [17592186321179 1111 #uuid "683c075e-6ddc-5536-b7a3-c5784c6694e0" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321180 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=-k(150-N)" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321180 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534939 true] [17592186321180 1111 #uuid "dc297bd0-0a39-50b2-bd9f-fe4520631b84" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321181 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k{N}^{2}(150-N)" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321181 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534939 true] [17592186321181 1111 #uuid "fb52132d-5dcd-5e01-8089-3e69edc3bccc" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321182 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N)" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321182 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534939 true] [17592186321182 1111 #uuid "f6654b41-f50b-5db7-983c-da59dc98c8aa" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321183 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321184 13194139534939 true] [17592186321183 1089 "q-335142" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321183 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534939 true] [17592186321183 1100 "A tutoring center currently has $10$ enrolled students. The number of students in the center $T(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the tutoring center is $95$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the student population of the center? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139534939 true] [17592186321183 1113 #uuid "ada01d1e-eba3-5928-91b8-5bffc1a1234f" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321183 1145 "Since the number of students in the center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of students changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of students in the center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the tutoring center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=95$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(95-T).\n$$" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321185 13194139534939 true] [17592186321184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321186 13194139534939 true] [17592186321184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321187 13194139534939 true] [17592186321184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321188 13194139534939 true] [17592186321184 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321189 13194139534939 true] [17592186321184 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534939 true] [17592186321184 1041 "selection" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321184 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321188 13194139534939 true] [17592186321184 1110 #uuid "f55e52d6-de0c-5f14-a216-0618ad1d3457" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321185 1034 "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(100-T)" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534939 true] [17592186321185 1111 #uuid "c98f81a1-56d4-5e26-bb4d-810ad98de381" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321186 1034 "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(30-T)" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321186 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534939 true] [17592186321186 1111 #uuid "c023f6c2-c807-555b-ace6-f7d5d9bf9288" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321187 1034 "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(60-T)" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534939 true] [17592186321187 1111 #uuid "62983bd2-81f7-5b29-b872-3282859bf573" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321188 1034 "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(95-T)" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534939 true] [17592186321188 1111 #uuid "ab1f1522-91c4-5a8b-94c6-2736e1f48ed2" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321189 1034 "\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(950-T)" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321189 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534939 true] [17592186321189 1111 #uuid "59433e00-c088-57f7-8813-b8d07339c866" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321190 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321191 13194139534939 true] [17592186321190 1089 "q-335280" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321190 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534939 true] [17592186321190 1100 "A can of soda is taken out of a refrigerator and left in a room that stays at a constant temperature of $20{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ The soda’s temperature $T(t)$ (in degrees Celsius) can be modeled by the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\ge 0$ is the time, measured in minutes, and $k>0$ is a constant. The initial temperature of the soda is $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and after $5$ minutes, the soda has warmed to $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the soda to reach $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}?$" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321190 1113 #uuid "cad98f12-8da4-54c8-87ad-6af552687ea0" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321190 1145 "We're told that the temperature of the can is modeled by the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=k(20-T).\n$$\n\nWe separate variables and integrate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{20-T} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{20-T} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|20-T| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $20-T>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20-T & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 20-T & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 20-T & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was $8{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=8,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =20-K{e}^{0} \\\\ 8 & =20-K \\\\ K & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=20-12{e}^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the temperature of the soda was $12{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=12,$  we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =20-12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 8 & =12{e}^{-5k} \\\\ {e}^{-5k} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -5k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$  be the time when the soda can reaches $16{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =16 \\\\ 20-12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =16 \\\\ 4 & =12{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the value of $k,$ we get:\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{3})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})}=13.547…≈14\\text{ }\\text{minutes},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." 13194139534939 true] [17592186321191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321192 13194139534939 true] [17592186321191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321193 13194139534939 true] [17592186321191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321194 13194139534939 true] [17592186321191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321195 13194139534939 true] [17592186321191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321196 13194139534939 true] [17592186321191 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534939 true] [17592186321191 1041 "selection" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321191 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321195 13194139534939 true] [17592186321191 1110 #uuid "ec52a582-0b36-573b-9119-43c61aefe9f8" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321192 1034 "$12$ minutes" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321192 1111 #uuid "61e1cd94-0168-537b-9253-806228ba636d" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321193 1034 "$19$ minutes" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321193 1111 #uuid "6258d3ee-665e-517c-892c-53b9d2e82a18" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321194 1034 "$8$ minutes" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321194 1111 #uuid "6507e808-0c0b-53ab-83f4-e951283a1a8d" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321195 1034 "$14$ minutes" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321195 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321195 1111 #uuid "9d9f460f-a72b-51cf-98a5-9f463a191fe1" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321196 1034 "$10$ minutes" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321196 1111 #uuid "b080d755-4f53-5aaa-b708-48dd41959f72" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321197 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321198 13194139534939 true] [17592186321197 1089 "q-335590" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321197 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534939 true] [17592186321197 1100 "A small bakery produces loaves of bread. The owner knows that the bakery can produce a maximum of $600$ loaves a day; however, current production is below this maximum. Since she started monitoring, the bakers produced $480$ loaves and $500$ loaves on days $4$ and $8,$ respectively. How many loaves do they produce on day $40?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the bread production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321197 1113 #uuid "ee0a87d3-d3e9-539a-9074-68a374d85d6b" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321197 1145 "The rate at which the bread production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible bread production and its current bread production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the bakery, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ loaves, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(600-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{600-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{600-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $4,$ the bread production was $480$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(4)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & =480 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $8,$ the bread production was $500$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(8)=500,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(8) & =500 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-8k} & =500 \\\\ K{e}^{-8k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-8k}} & =\\frac{120}{100} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} & =120 \\\\ K & =120{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} \\\\ K & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=600-144{e}^{-(t/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of loaves baked on day $t=40$ is\n\n$$\nE(40)=600-144{e}^{-(40/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}≈576.743…≈577\\,\\text{loaves},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139534939 true] [17592186321198 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321199 13194139534939 true] [17592186321198 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321200 13194139534939 true] [17592186321198 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321201 13194139534939 true] [17592186321198 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321202 13194139534939 true] [17592186321198 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321203 13194139534939 true] [17592186321198 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534939 true] [17592186321198 1041 "selection" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321198 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321201 13194139534939 true] [17592186321198 1110 #uuid "ea96f7c0-be13-5f40-994f-df9e4b8563dd" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321199 1034 "$570$ loaves" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321199 1111 #uuid "bffc4b47-de4e-552a-81b4-6adec40f886f" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321200 1034 "$560$ loaves" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321200 1111 #uuid "c4578f9e-b258-5042-b524-dde82d917182" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321201 1034 "$577$ loaves" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321201 1111 #uuid "54511ca5-bc2d-5ea2-bdc1-07a9c508099f" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321202 1034 "$550$ loaves" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321202 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321202 1111 #uuid "66012f87-f092-5ae2-84e1-b8c4819eb86d" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321203 1034 "$590$ loaves" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321203 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321203 1111 #uuid "22041062-30ce-51ce-9748-f3e2cadeebf8" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321204 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321205 13194139534939 true] [17592186321204 1089 "q-335619" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321204 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534939 true] [17592186321204 1100 "A national park is monitoring the number of people who have downloaded a new park app. The system can support a maximum of $800$ users, but the total is still below this limit. Since the park began tracking downloads, the number of users reached $620$ on day $3$ and $720$ on day $9.$ On what day will the number of users reach $760?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of users, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321204 1113 #uuid "44cded13-6bc7-52ea-8fcc-d6fe583c3d07" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321204 1145 "The rate at which the number of app users $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of users the system can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ users, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the number of users was $620.$ So, applying the condition $P(3)=620,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =620 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-3k} & =620 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the number of users was $720.$ So, applying the condition $P(9)=720,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =720 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-9k} & =720 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{180}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} & =180 \\\\ K & =180{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} \\\\ K & =180\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ K & =180\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-270{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$  be the day when the app reaches $760$ users. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =760 \\\\ 800-270{e}^{-kd} & =760 \\\\ 40 & =270{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{40}{270} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{4}{27} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the number of users will be $760$ on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})}=14.1285…≈14,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day." 13194139534939 true] [17592186321205 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321206 13194139534939 true] [17592186321205 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321207 13194139534939 true] [17592186321205 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321208 13194139534939 true] [17592186321205 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321209 13194139534939 true] [17592186321205 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321210 13194139534939 true] [17592186321205 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534939 true] [17592186321205 1041 "selection" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321205 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321207 13194139534939 true] [17592186321205 1110 #uuid "002cce17-781a-5345-9b29-bcfe6067a58e" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321206 1034 "$25\\text{th}$ day" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321206 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321206 1111 #uuid "7aa49a75-0f3f-550f-9d46-14ceefd1ae98" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321207 1034 "$14\\text{th}$ day" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321207 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534939 true] [17592186321207 1111 #uuid "2fe184fd-190b-5065-afb0-8cb5d9f91f50" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321208 1034 "$12\\text{th}$ day" 13194139534939 true] [17592186321208 11